0:00:00.400,0:00:10.000
Two-handed pinochle, start to finish, in one hand.

0:00:10.000,0:00:15.600
You and the computer, Dix, each hold 12 cards.
You score two ways: by laying down melds,

0:00:15.600,0:00:22.880
which are card combinations, and by winning
tricks that hold aces, tens, and kings.

0:00:22.880,0:00:26.640
The card turned up under the stock
sets trump for the deal. Here it's

0:00:26.640,0:00:37.040
clubs. Any trump beats every
card of the other three suits.

0:00:37.040,0:00:41.040
Cards rank ace, ten, king, queen, jack, nine. Yes,

0:00:41.040,0:00:51.280
the ten beats the king. To win a trick, play
a higher card of the same suit, or any trump.

0:00:51.280,0:00:56.720
Cards you capture score points. An ace is
11, a ten is 10, a king 4, a queen 3,

0:00:56.720,0:01:04.480
and a jack 2. After each trick, both
players draw a card, and the winner leads next.

0:01:04.480,0:01:08.320
Win a trick and you may lay down
one meld. A king and queen of trump

0:01:08.320,0:01:17.840
make a royal marriage, worth 40 points.
Melded cards stay yours to play later.

0:01:32.480,0:01:42.400
Dix melded too. Its meld lies face up, so
now you know some of the cards in its hand.

0:02:17.360,0:02:21.520
When the stock runs out, the playoff
begins. Your melds go back in your

0:02:21.520,0:02:36.400
hand, and now you must follow suit.
If you can't, you must play a trump.

0:03:00.800,0:03:05.040
That's a deal. Your score is your melds
plus your trick points. In the full game,

0:03:05.040,0:03:16.720
deals continue until someone reaches 1,000.

0:03:16.720,0:03:20.160
Pinochle: Two-Handed. Free to try on the App Store.
